entry .Lsp   the protocol: its types, the handler registry, `listen`, `proxy`, and the client half
  detail
      `Lsp` is two things which share a name. As a **trait**, it is the full Language Server
      Protocol wire surface — the union of `LspLifecycle`, `LspLanguage`, `LspNavigation`,
      `LspEditing`, `LspAdvanced`, `LspWorkspace` and `LspResolve`, mixed with obligatory's
      `JsonRpc` and fixing `#Origin` to `Lsp.Client` — which `Lsp.listen`'s session implements
      and `Lsp.Dispatch` serves. An implementation of one's own is possible (`dispatcher`
      routes it) but the registration API is the intended way to build a server.

      As an **object**, it is the vocabulary. Three groups of members live here:

      - the **protocol types**, one case class or enum per LSP structure, each with a JSON
        codec — `Position`, `Range`, `Location`, `Diagnostic`, `Hover`, `CompletionItem`,
        `CompletionList`, `TextEdit`, `WorkspaceEdit`, `ServerCapabilities`, `SymbolKind`,
        `DiagnosticSeverity` and the rest;
      - the **server side**: the handler types (`Focused`, `Ambient`, `HoverHandler`, …), the
        ambient accessors a handler reads (`document`, `workspace`, `client`, `position`, …),
        the registration combinators (`hover`, `complete`, `opened`, `command`, …), the
        `Registry` they write to, `listen`, which serves, and `Error`, which a handler raises;
      - the **client side**: `Server`, which describes a server to speak to, `Connection`,
        the handle a session lends, `Listener` and `Observer`, and `Client`, the
        notifications a server sends its editor; and `Proxy` and `proxy`, which put one
        process between an editor and a server.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object Features:
        import Lsp.*

        def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
          hover:
            document.word(position).let: word =>
              Hover(MarkupContent(value = t"**$word** at line ${position.line}"))

          definition(Nil)

          command(t"demo.reverse"):
            arguments.let(_.head).let: argument =>
              val text: Text = argument.as[Text]
              Text(StringBuilder(text.s).reverse.toString).in[Json]
      ```

      Registration is possible wherever an `Lsp.Registry^` is in scope — the block of
      `listen`, or a helper such as `register` above which takes one — so a large server
      splits its features across methods.

      Import `Lsp.*` inside the object which uses it: several of its members (`document`,
      `context`, `arguments`, `query`, `Document`) collide with other libraries' names in
      the `soundness` umbrella, which is why the umbrella exports `Lsp` and not its contents.
  refer exegesis.Lsp.listen
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Registry
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Connection
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Server
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Error
  entry #Origin   the far end of the protocol from a server's point of view: `Lsp.Client`
      The `JsonRpc` origin type: the interface whose methods an `Lsp` implementation may
      call back on the peer which sent it a message. A server's `client` handle is one.
  entry .Position   a line and a character, both zero-based, in UTF-16 code units
    detail
        The protocol's position: `line` and `character` count from zero, and `character` is
        an offset in UTF-16 code units, the protocol's default encoding and the unit of
        `Text`'s underlying characters, so no transcoding happens anywhere in the library.
        `Document#offset` and `Document#position` convert between a position and an offset
        into a document's text, clamping as the protocol requires. `#span` and `.from` convert
        to and from zephyrine's `Span`, which is zero-based too.
    entry .from   a position from the start of a `Span`, or `Unset` if the span has no line
        Takes the span's start line and start column (zero when absent).
    entry #line   the zero-based line
    entry #character   the zero-based offset within the line, in UTF-16 code units
    entry #span   the position as a zero-width `Span` on its line
  entry .Range   two positions, the start inclusive and the end exclusive
    detail
        The protocol's range. `Document#apply(range)` reads the text within one, and
        `Document#fullRange` is the range of a whole document. `.from` converts a `Span`,
        using the start line and column for a missing end, and `#span` converts back. The
        companion's `encodable` and `decodable` are the JSON codec, derived once here so
        every use shares it.
    entry .from   a range from a `Span`, or `Unset` if the span has no start line
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder for a range
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder for a range; a malformed range throws `Json.Error`
    entry #start   where the range begins
    entry #end   where the range ends, exclusive
    entry #span   the range as a `Span` over its area
  entry .Location   a range within a document named by URI
    detail
        What `definition`, `declaration`, `references`, `typeDefinition` and `implementation`
        return a list of. The companion holds the derived JSON codec.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed location throws `Json.Error`
    entry #uri   the document's URI, as the editor spells it (`file:///…`)
    entry #range   the range within that document
  entry .Envelope   the two members of any JSON-RPC message which decide its handling
    detail
        A message read only for its `method`, which a response lacks, and its `id`, which a
        notification lacks. Both are optional, so every message decodes, and a proxy uses
        one to classify a message without knowing its shape: no method means a response, no
        id means a notification.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder
    entry #method   the method the message names, or `Unset` for a response
    entry #id   the id the message correlates on, as raw JSON, or `Unset` for a notification
  entry .TextDocumentIdentifier   a document named by its URI
    entry #uri   the document's URI
  entry .VersionedTextDocumentIdentifier   a document named by its URI and its version
    detail
        What `textDocument/didChange` carries: the version is the editor's count of edits,
        which the session records as `Document#version`.
    entry #uri   the document's URI
    entry #version   the editor's version number for the document
  entry .TextDocumentItem   an open document in full: URI, language, version and text
    detail
        What `textDocument/didOpen` carries, and what `Workspace#document` returns as a
        snapshot of a document the session holds.
    entry #uri   the document's URI
    entry #languageId   the editor's language identifier, such as `scala` or `rust`
    entry #version   the editor's version number for the document
    entry #text   the document's whole text
  entry .TextDocumentContentChangeEvent   one edit: a range replaced by text, or the whole text replaced
    detail
        An event with a `range` splices the text at the range's UTF-16 offsets; one without
        replaces the whole document, which is how an editor using full synchronisation
        reports every change. `Connection#edit` sends a list of these, and `Connection#replace`
        sends the unranged form.
    entry #range   the range replaced, or `Unset` to replace the whole document
    entry #text   the replacement text
  entry .ClientInfo   the editor's name and version, as reported at initialization
    entry #name   the editor's name
    entry #version   the editor's version, if it gave one
  entry .ServerInfo   the server's name and version, as announced in the `initialize` result
    detail
        `Lsp.listen(name, version)` sends these; `Connection#initialize` returns them inside
        the `InitializeResult`.
    entry #name   the server's name
    entry #version   the server's version, if it gave one
  entry .Folder   a workspace folder: its URI and its display name
    entry #uri   the folder's URI
    entry #name   the folder's display name
  entry .CompletionOptions   how a server completes: trigger characters and whether items resolve
    detail
        Derived by `Registry#capabilities` from a `complete(triggers*)` registration, with
        `resolveProvider` set when `resolveCompletion` was registered too.
    entry #triggerCharacters   characters whose typing asks for completion, if any
    entry #resolveProvider   whether `completionItem/resolve` is served
  entry .SignatureHelpOptions   the characters which trigger signature help
    entry #triggerCharacters   the trigger characters, if any
  entry .DocumentLinkOptions   whether document links resolve lazily
    entry #resolveProvider   whether `documentLink/resolve` is served
  entry .CodeLensOptions   whether code lenses resolve lazily
    entry #resolveProvider   whether `codeLens/resolve` is served
  entry .DocumentOnTypeFormattingOptions   the characters whose typing triggers formatting
    detail
        Derived from `formatOnType(first, more*)`: the protocol requires one trigger character
        and allows more.
    entry #firstTriggerCharacter   the character which must trigger formatting
    entry #moreTriggerCharacter   further trigger characters, if any
  entry .SemanticTokensOptions   the legend a server's semantic tokens use, and which requests it serves
    detail
        Derived from `semanticTokens(legend)`, with `full` set when that handler exists and
        `range` when `semanticTokensRange` was registered.
    entry #legend   the token types and modifiers the server's token data indexes into
    entry #range   whether `textDocument/semanticTokens/range` is served
    entry #full   whether `textDocument/semanticTokens/full` is served
  entry .DiagnosticOptions   how a server's pull diagnostics behave
    detail
        The options `diagnostics(options)` takes at registration: the protocol requires a
        server to say whether a document's diagnostics depend on other files and whether it
        can report for the whole workspace.
    entry #identifier   an identifier under which the client keeps the results, if any
    entry #interFileDependencies   whether a change in one file may change another's diagnostics
    entry #workspaceDiagnostics   whether the server reports diagnostics for the whole workspace
  entry .ExecuteCommandOptions   the commands a server executes
    detail
        Derived from every `command(name)` registration; absent when there are none.
    entry #commands   the registered command names
  entry .ServerCapabilities   what a server tells its editor it can do
    detail
        The record sent in the `initialize` result. A server built with `Lsp.listen` never
        writes one: `Registry#capabilities` derives it from the registered handlers, each
        provider flag set exactly when its handler exists, with `textDocumentSync` always
        `Incremental`, and the `capabilities(adjust)` registration amends the derived record
        last. A proxy amends the upstream server's record with `rewrite.capabilities`, and a
        client reads one from `Connection#initialize`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val capabilities: Lsp.ServerCapabilities =
          Lsp.ServerCapabilities(hoverProvider = true, definitionProvider = true)

        capabilities.copy(completionProvider = Lsp.CompletionOptions(triggerCharacters = List(t".")))
        ```

        Every field is optional and defaults to `Unset`, which the codec omits.
    entry #textDocumentSync   how the server wants document changes reported
    entry #completionProvider   completion, with its options
    entry #hoverProvider   hover
    entry #definitionProvider   go to definition
    entry #referencesProvider   find references
    entry #documentSymbolProvider   document symbols
    entry #documentFormattingProvider   whole-document formatting
    entry #renameProvider   rename
    entry #codeActionProvider   code actions
    entry #signatureHelpProvider   signature help, with its trigger characters
    entry #declarationProvider   go to declaration
    entry #typeDefinitionProvider   go to type definition
    entry #implementationProvider   go to implementation
    entry #documentHighlightProvider   document highlights
    entry #foldingRangeProvider   folding ranges
    entry #selectionRangeProvider   selection ranges
    entry #colorProvider   document colours and colour presentations
    entry #documentRangeFormattingProvider   range formatting
    entry #documentLinkProvider   document links, with their options
    entry #codeLensProvider   code lenses, with their options
    entry #documentOnTypeFormattingProvider   on-type formatting, with its trigger characters
    entry #callHierarchyProvider   call hierarchy
    entry #typeHierarchyProvider   type hierarchy
    entry #semanticTokensProvider   semantic tokens, with their legend
    entry #inlayHintProvider   inlay hints
    entry #inlineValueProvider   inline values
    entry #linkedEditingRangeProvider   linked editing ranges
    entry #monikerProvider   monikers
    entry #diagnosticProvider   pull diagnostics, with their options
    entry #workspaceSymbolProvider   workspace symbols
    entry #executeCommandProvider   commands, with their names
  entry .InitializeResult   the server's answer to `initialize`: its capabilities and its identity
    detail
        Returned by `Connection#initialize`, and the payload `rewrite.capabilities` amends
        in a proxy. The companion holds the derived JSON codec.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed result throws `Json.Error`
    entry #capabilities   what the server can do
    entry #serverInfo   the server's name and version, if it gave them
  entry .MarkupContent   text for display, Markdown unless its kind says otherwise
    detail
        What a `Hover` and a `CompletionItem`'s documentation carry. The `kind` defaults to
        `markdown`; `plaintext` is the other value the protocol knows.
    entry #kind   `markdown` (the default) or `plaintext`
    entry #value   the text
  entry .CompletionContext   why the editor asked for completion
    detail
        The `triggerKind` is the protocol's number: 1 for an explicit request, 2 for a trigger
        character (then given in `triggerCharacter`), 3 for a re-request of an incomplete list.
        A `complete` handler reads it as `context`, which is `Unset` when the editor sent none.
    entry #triggerKind   the protocol's number for the kind of trigger
    entry #triggerCharacter   the character typed, when a trigger character asked for completion
  entry .CompletionItem   one completion: its label, kind, detail, documentation and insert text
    detail
        What a `CompletionList` holds, and what `resolveCompletion` fills in lazily: a server
        which computes documentation on demand returns a bare item from `complete` and
        completes it in `resolveCompletion`, carrying whatever it needs in `data`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Lsp.CompletionItem(label = t"soundness", kind = Lsp.CompletionItemKind.Keyword)
        ```
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed item throws `Json.Error`
    entry #label   the text shown in the list, and inserted unless `insertText` says otherwise
    entry #kind   the item's kind, which the editor renders as an icon
    entry #detail   a short type or signature, shown beside the label
    entry #documentation   longer documentation, as markup
    entry #insertText   the text inserted, when it differs from the label
    entry #data   anything the server wants back at `completionItem/resolve`, as raw JSON
  entry .CompletionList   the items a completion request returns, and whether there are more
    detail
        What a `complete` handler returns. `isIncomplete` tells the editor to ask again as the
        user types rather than filtering the list it has.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed list throws `Json.Error`
    entry #isIncomplete   whether further typing should ask the server again
    entry #items   the completions
  entry .Hover   what a hover shows: markup, and the range it applies to
    detail
        What a `hover` handler returns inside an `Optional`: `Unset` means nothing to show,
        and goes on the wire as `null`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed hover throws `Json.Error`
    entry #contents   the markup shown
    entry #range   the range the hover describes, which the editor may highlight, if given
  entry .ReferenceContext   whether a references request wants the declaration included
    entry #includeDeclaration   whether the symbol's own declaration counts as a reference
  entry .DocumentSymbol   a symbol in a document, with its kind, ranges and children
    detail
        What a `documentSymbols` handler returns a list of, and the hierarchical form of the
        protocol's symbol (as against the flat `WorkspaceSymbol`): a symbol nests its
        `children`, and its `range` encloses the whole definition while `selectionRange`
        picks out its name.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed symbol throws `Json.Error`
    entry #name   the symbol's name
    entry #kind   the kind of symbol
    entry #range   the whole definition
    entry #selectionRange   the symbol's name within the definition
    entry #detail   a type or signature, if any
    entry #children   nested symbols, if any
  entry .FormattingOptions   the editor's formatting preferences: tab size and spaces or tabs
    entry #tabSize   the width of a tab
    entry #insertSpaces   whether indentation uses spaces rather than tabs
  entry .TextEdit   a range of a document replaced by new text
    detail
        What `format`, `formatRange`, `formatOnType` and `savingEdits` return lists of, and
        what a `WorkspaceEdit` holds per document.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed edit throws `Json.Error`
    entry #range   the range replaced
    entry #newText   the replacement text
  entry .WorkspaceEdit   edits across documents, keyed by URI
    detail
        What `rename` returns, and what the `creatingFiles`, `renamingFiles` and
        `deletingFiles` handlers may return to amend a file operation before it happens.
        Only the `changes` form of the protocol's edit is modelled, not document changes with
        versions.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed edit throws `Json.Error`
    entry #changes   the edits to each document, by URI, if any
  entry .CodeActionContext   the diagnostics at the range a code action was requested for
    detail
        A `codeActions` handler reads it as `context`; a quick fix is usually computed from
        these diagnostics.
    entry #diagnostics   the diagnostics the editor shows at the range
  entry .CodeAction   a titled action the editor may offer: an edit, under a kind
    detail
        What a `codeActions` handler returns a list of. The `kind` is a dotted protocol string
        such as `quickfix` or `refactor.extract`; `edit` may be left `Unset` and filled in by
        `resolveCodeAction`, carrying what it needs in `data`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed action throws `Json.Error`
    entry #title   the title shown to the user
    entry #kind   the action's kind, such as `quickfix`
    entry #edit   the edit the action applies, if computed
    entry #data   anything the server wants back at `codeAction/resolve`, as raw JSON
  entry .ParameterInformation   one parameter of a signature, by its label
    entry #label   the parameter's label, as it appears in the signature
  entry .SignatureInformation   one signature: its label, documentation and parameters
    entry #label   the signature, as text
    entry #documentation   documentation, as markup, if any
    entry #parameters   the parameters, if listed
  entry .SignatureHelp   the signatures at a call site, and which is active
    detail
        What a `signatureHelp` handler returns inside an `Optional`: `Unset` means nothing to
        show.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #signatures   the candidate signatures
    entry #activeSignature   the index of the signature to show first, if any
  entry .DocumentHighlight   a range to highlight, as a read, a write or plain text
    detail
        What a `documentHighlights` handler returns a list of: every occurrence of the symbol
        under the cursor.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed highlight throws `Json.Error`
    entry #range   the range to highlight
    entry #kind   whether the occurrence reads, writes, or is plain text
  entry .FoldingRange   a foldable region of lines, optionally of a kind
    detail
        What a `foldingRanges` handler returns a list of. The `kind` is a protocol string such
        as `comment`, `imports` or `region`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed range throws `Json.Error`
    entry #startLine   the first folded line
    entry #startCharacter   the character on the first line the fold begins at, if not the end
    entry #endLine   the last folded line
    entry #endCharacter   the character on the last line the fold ends at, if not the end
    entry #kind   `comment`, `imports`, `region`, or a kind of the server's own
    entry #collapsedText   text shown in place of the folded lines, if any
  entry .SelectionRange   a range and the enclosing range it expands to
    detail
        What a `selectionRanges` handler returns, one per position asked for: a chain of
        ever-wider ranges, each the `parent` of the last, which the editor's expand-selection
        command walks.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed range throws `Json.Error`
    entry #range   this range
    entry #parent   the next wider range, if any
  entry .Command   a titled command with arbitrary arguments, as a code lens or action carries one
    detail
        The `arguments` are the protocol's `LSPAny[]`, kept as raw `Json`. The editor runs
        the command by sending `workspace/executeCommand`, which a `command(name)`
        registration answers.
    entry #title   the title shown to the user
    entry #command   the command's name
    entry #arguments   the arguments, as a raw JSON array, if any
  entry .CodeLens   a command shown inline at a range
    detail
        What a `codeLenses` handler returns a list of. The `command` may be left `Unset` and
        filled in by `resolveCodeLens`, carrying what it needs in `data`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed lens throws `Json.Error`
    entry #range   where the lens is shown
    entry #command   the command the lens runs, if computed
    entry #data   anything the server wants back at `codeLens/resolve`, as raw JSON
  entry .DocumentLink   a range which links to a target
    detail
        What a `documentLinks` handler returns a list of. The `target` may be left `Unset`
        and filled in by `resolveDocumentLink`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed link throws `Json.Error`
    entry #range   the linked range
    entry #target   the URI the range links to, if computed
    entry #tooltip   text shown when hovering the link, if any
    entry #data   anything the server wants back at `documentLink/resolve`, as raw JSON
  entry .Color   a colour as red, green, blue and alpha, each from 0 to 1
    entry #red   the red component
    entry #green   the green component
    entry #blue   the blue component
    entry #alpha   the alpha component
  entry .ColorInformation   a colour literal in a document: its range and its value
    detail
        What a `documentColors` handler returns a list of; the editor shows a swatch at
        each.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #range   where the colour literal is
    entry #color   the colour it denotes
  entry .ColorPresentation   one way of writing a colour, as a labelled edit
    detail
        What a `colorPresentations` handler returns a list of: each is a spelling of the
        colour the user picked (`#ff0000`, `rgb(255, 0, 0)`, …) with the edit that writes it.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #label   the spelling shown to the user
    entry #textEdit   the edit which writes it, if it differs from inserting the label
    entry #additionalTextEdits   further edits, such as an import, if any
  entry .CallHierarchyItem   a callable in the call hierarchy: a symbol with its location
    detail
        What `callHierarchy`'s prepare handler returns a list of, and what its incoming and
        outgoing handlers receive as `item`. `data` carries whatever the server needs to find
        the callable again when the editor asks for its callers or callees.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed item throws `Json.Error`
    entry #name   the callable's name
    entry #kind   the kind of symbol
    entry #tags   the symbol's tags, such as deprecated, if any
    entry #detail   a signature or type, if any
    entry #uri   the document the callable is defined in
    entry #range   the whole definition
    entry #selectionRange   the callable's name within the definition
    entry #data   anything the server wants back with the item, as raw JSON
  entry .CallHierarchyIncomingCall   a caller of an item, and where in the caller the calls are
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #from   the calling item
    entry #fromRanges   the ranges within the caller where the calls happen
  entry .CallHierarchyOutgoingCall   a callee of an item, and where in the item the calls are
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #to   the called item
    entry #fromRanges   the ranges within the caller where the calls happen
  entry .TypeHierarchyItem   a type in the type hierarchy: a symbol with its location
    detail
        What `typeHierarchy`'s prepare handler returns a list of, and what its supertypes and
        subtypes handlers receive as `item`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed item throws `Json.Error`
    entry #name   the type's name
    entry #kind   the kind of symbol
    entry #tags   the symbol's tags, if any
    entry #detail   further detail, if any
    entry #uri   the document the type is defined in
    entry #range   the whole definition
    entry #selectionRange   the type's name within the definition
    entry #data   anything the server wants back with the item, as raw JSON
  entry .SemanticTokensLegend   the token types and modifiers a server's token data indexes into
    detail
        Given at registration, `semanticTokens(legend)`, and advertised in the capabilities:
        each token in `SemanticTokens#data` names its type and modifiers by index into these
        lists.
    entry #tokenTypes   the token types, in index order
    entry #tokenModifiers   the token modifiers, in bit order
  entry .SemanticTokens   a document's semantic tokens, as the protocol's flat integer array
    detail
        What `semanticTokens` and `semanticTokensRange` handlers return. `data` is the
        protocol's encoding: five integers per token — line delta, start-character delta,
        length, token type index, modifier bit set — relative to the previous token.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #resultId   an identifier for this result, which a later delta request names
    entry #data   the tokens, five integers each, relative to the previous token
  entry .SemanticTokensEdit   a splice into a previous token array
    entry #start   the index into the previous data at which the edit starts
    entry #deleteCount   how many integers are removed
    entry #data   the integers inserted, if any
  entry .SemanticTokensDelta   the edits which turn a previous token array into the current one
    detail
        What a `semanticTokensDelta` handler returns, given `previousResultId`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #resultId   an identifier for the result after the edits
    entry #edits   the splices to apply
  entry .InlayHint   a label shown inline at a position, such as an inferred type
    detail
        What an `inlayHints` handler returns a list of. `tooltip` may be left `Unset` and
        filled in by `resolveInlayHint`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed hint throws `Json.Error`
    entry #position   where the hint is shown
    entry #label   the hint's text
    entry #kind   whether the hint is a type or a parameter name
    entry #tooltip   text shown when hovering the hint, if any
    entry #paddingLeft   whether to pad before the hint
    entry #paddingRight   whether to pad after the hint
    entry #data   anything the server wants back at `inlayHint/resolve`, as raw JSON
  entry .InlineValueContext   the debugger's state an inline-value request is made under
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #frameId   the stack frame the debugger is stopped in
    entry #stoppedLocation   the range of the statement the debugger is stopped at
  entry .InlineValueText   a value shown inline at a range while debugging
    detail
        What an `inlineValues` handler returns a list of. Only the literal-text form of the
        protocol's inline value is modelled.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #range   where the value is shown
    entry #text   the text shown
  entry .LinkedEditingRanges   ranges which are edited together, such as a tag's two names
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #ranges   the ranges which change together
    entry #wordPattern   a regular expression the linked text must match, if any
  entry .Moniker   a stable identity for a symbol across projects
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed moniker throws `Json.Error`
    entry #scheme   the moniker scheme, such as `tsc` or `.NET`
    entry #identifier   the identifier within the scheme
    entry #unique   how unique the moniker is: `document`, `project`, `group`, `scheme` or `global`
    entry #kind   `import`, `export` or `local`, if given
  entry .DocumentDiagnosticReport   the pull-diagnostics answer for one document
    detail
        What a `diagnostics` handler returns, and `Connection#diagnostics` asks for. Only the
        `full` kind is modelled: `kind` defaults to it, and an unchanged report is expressed by
        returning the same `resultId`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed report throws `Json.Error`
    entry #kind   `full`, the default
    entry #resultId   an identifier for this result, which a later request names
    entry #items   the diagnostics
  entry .WorkspaceSymbol   a symbol anywhere in the workspace, with its location
    detail
        What a `workspaceSymbols` handler returns a list of, in answer to a `query`; the flat
        counterpart of `DocumentSymbol`. `resolveWorkspaceSymbol` may fill in the location
        lazily.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed symbol throws `Json.Error`
    entry #name   the symbol's name
    entry #kind   the kind of symbol
    entry #tags   the symbol's tags, if any
    entry #location   where the symbol is
    entry #containerName   the name of the symbol's container, if any
    entry #data   anything the server wants back at `workspaceSymbol/resolve`, as raw JSON
  entry .FileEvent   a watched file which was created, changed or deleted
    detail
        What a `watchedFiles` handler receives a list of as `changes`. The field is spelled
        `` `type` `` in Scala, as in the protocol.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed event throws `Json.Error`
    entry #uri   the file's URI
    entry #type   whether it was created, changed or deleted
  entry .WorkspaceFoldersChangeEvent   folders added to and removed from the workspace
    detail
        What a `foldersChanged` handler receives as `event`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed event throws `Json.Error`
    entry #added   the folders added
    entry #removed   the folders removed
  entry .FileCreate   a file about to be, or just, created
    detail
        What `creatingFiles` and `createdFiles` handlers receive a list of as `files`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #uri   the file's URI
  entry .FileRename   a file about to be, or just, renamed
    detail
        What `renamingFiles` and `renamedFiles` handlers receive a list of as `files`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #oldUri   the URI before the rename
    entry #newUri   the URI after it
  entry .FileDelete   a file about to be, or just, deleted
    detail
        What `deletingFiles` and `deletedFiles` handlers receive a list of as `files`.
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed value throws `Json.Error`
    entry #uri   the file's URI
  entry .Diagnostic   a problem at a range: its severity, code, source and message
    detail
        What a server publishes with `client.publishDiagnostics`, returns from a `diagnostics`
        handler, and what reaches a client's `Listener#diagnostics`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Lsp.Diagnostic
          ( range    = Lsp.Range(Lsp.Position(0, 0), Lsp.Position(0, 5)),
            severity = Lsp.DiagnosticSeverity.Warning,
            message  = t"unused import" )
        ```
    entry .encodable   the derived JSON encoder
    entry .decodable   the derived JSON decoder; a malformed diagnostic throws `Json.Error`
    entry #range   where the problem is
    entry #severity   error, warning, information or hint, if given
    entry #code   the problem's code, as text, if any
    entry #source   what reported it, such as a compiler's name, if given
    entry #message   the message shown to the user
  entry .MessageActionItem   one button of a `window/showMessageRequest`, by its title
    entry #title   the button's title
  entry .DiagnosticSeverity   how serious a diagnostic is: `Error`, `Warning`, `Information` or `Hint`
    detail
        An integer-valued protocol enum: numbered from 1 on the wire, so `Error` encodes as
        `1` and `Warning` as `2`. The companion's `encodable` and `decodable` are the codec;
        the decoder takes the caller's `Tactic[Json.Error]` and captures it, so decoding one
        needs a tactic in scope, and a wire integer outside the range raises through it.
    entry .encodable   encodes the severity as its protocol number, from 1
    entry .decodable   decodes a protocol number, raising `Json.Error` through the caller's tactic
  entry .MessageType   the level of a window or log message: `Error`, `Warning`, `Info` or `Log`
    detail
        Numbered from 1 on the wire. `Client#showMessage` sends `Info` and `Client#logMessage`
        sends `Log`; the wire methods take any level. The decoder captures the caller's
        `Tactic[Json.Error]`.
    entry .encodable   encodes the level as its protocol number, from 1
    entry .decodable   decodes a protocol number, raising `Json.Error` through the caller's tactic
  entry .TextDocumentSyncKind   how document changes are reported: `None`, `Full` or `Incremental`
    detail
        The one enum numbered from 0 on the wire, so `None` is `0`, `Full` is `1` and
        `Incremental` is `2`. A server built with `Lsp.listen` always advertises
        `Incremental`; a document's state is kept correct under either form of change event.
        The decoder captures the caller's `Tactic[Json.Error]`.
    entry .encodable   encodes the kind as its protocol number, from 0
    entry .decodable   decodes a protocol number, raising `Json.Error` through the caller's tactic
  entry .CompletionItemKind   the kind of a completion: `Text`, `Method`, `Function`, …, `TypeParameter`
    detail
        The protocol's twenty-five kinds, in wire order from 1: `Text`, `Method`, `Function`,
        `Constructor`, `Field`, `Variable`, `Class`, `Interface`, `Module`, `Property`, `Unit`,
        `Value`, `Enum`, `Keyword`, `Snippet`, `Color`, `File`, `Reference`, `Folder`,
        `EnumMember`, `Constant`, `Struct`, `Event`, `Operator`, `TypeParameter`. Some share
        their names with Scala types (`Text`, `Unit`), which is harmless as enum cases but
        means `CompletionItemKind.Unit` must be qualified. The decoder captures the caller's
        `Tactic[Json.Error]`.
    entry .encodable   encodes the kind as its protocol number, from 1
    entry .decodable   decodes a protocol number, raising `Json.Error` through the caller's tactic
  entry .SymbolKind   the kind of a symbol: `File`, `Module`, `Namespace`, `Package`, `Class`, …, `TypeParameter`
    detail
        The protocol's twenty-six kinds, in wire order from 1: `File`, `Module`, `Namespace`,
        `Package`, `Class`, `Method`, `Property`, `Field`, `Constructor`, `Enum`, `Interface`,
        `Function`, `Variable`, `Constant`, `String`, `Number`, `Boolean`, `Array`, `Object`,
        `Key`, `Null`, `EnumMember`, `Struct`, `Event`, `Operator`, `TypeParameter`. The cases
        which name Scala types are written with backticks in the source and must be
        qualified (`SymbolKind.String`). The decoder captures the caller's `Tactic[Json.Error]`.
    entry .encodable   encodes the kind as its protocol number, from 1
    entry .decodable   decodes a protocol number, raising `Json.Error` through the caller's tactic
  entry .DocumentHighlightKind   what an occurrence does: `Text`, `Read` or `Write`
    detail
        Numbered from 1 on the wire. The decoder captures the caller's `Tactic[Json.Error]`.
    entry .encodable   encodes the kind as its protocol number, from 1
    entry .decodable   decodes a protocol number, raising `Json.Error` through the caller's tactic
  entry .TextDocumentSaveReason   why a document is being saved: `Manual`, `AfterDelay` or `FocusOut`
    detail
        What the `saving` and `savingEdits` handlers read as `reason`. Numbered from 1 on
        the wire. The decoder captures the caller's `Tactic[Json.Error]`.
    entry .encodable   encodes the reason as its protocol number, from 1
    entry .decodable   decodes a protocol number, raising `Json.Error` through the caller's tactic
  entry .SymbolTag   a tag on a symbol; only `Deprecated` is defined
    detail
        Numbered from 1 on the wire. The decoder captures the caller's `Tactic[Json.Error]`.
    entry .encodable   encodes the tag as its protocol number, from 1
    entry .decodable   decodes a protocol number, raising `Json.Error` through the caller's tactic
  entry .InlayHintKind   what an inlay hint shows: a `Type` or a `Parameter` name
    detail
        Numbered from 1 on the wire. The decoder captures the caller's `Tactic[Json.Error]`.
    entry .encodable   encodes the kind as its protocol number, from 1
    entry .decodable   decodes a protocol number, raising `Json.Error` through the caller's tactic
  entry .FileChangeType   what happened to a watched file: `Created`, `Changed` or `Deleted`
    detail
        The `type` of a `FileEvent`. Numbered from 1 on the wire. The decoder captures the
        caller's `Tactic[Json.Error]`.
    entry .encodable   encodes the change as its protocol number, from 1
    entry .decodable   decodes a protocol number, raising `Json.Error` through the caller's tactic
  entry .Document   a live view of the open document a handler concerns, lent for one dispatch
    detail
        The handle a document-scoped handler reads as `document`: the text the editor has
        reported so far for the document the request names, with incremental changes applied
        at UTF-16 offsets, plus line access, conversion between protocol positions and
        offsets, and the word under a position. It is an exclusive capability, lent for the
        duration of one handler and confined to it — assigning `document` to an outer
        variable, or capturing it in a task, is a compile error — while every value it
        returns (`Text`, `Position`, `Range`) is a pure snapshot which may leave.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        object Features:
          import Lsp.*

          def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
            hover:
              val line: Optional[Text] = document.line(position.line)
              val word: Optional[Text] = document.word(position)
              word.let { word => Hover(MarkupContent(value = t"`$word` in ${document.uri}")) }
        ```

        Positions and offsets are in UTF-16 code units, which is both the protocol's default
        position encoding and the unit of `Text`'s characters. A position beyond the end of
        the text or of its line is clamped, as the protocol requires. Lines are delimited by
        `\n`, `\r\n` or `\r`.

        A request naming a document the editor never opened reaches no handler: the session
        records an `Lsp.Error` with reason `InvalidParams`, _the document `uri` is not open_,
        and answers with it.
    refer exegesis.Lsp.Workspace
    refer exegesis.Lsp.document
    entry #uri   the document's URI
    entry #language   the editor's language identifier for the document
    entry #version   the version the editor last reported
    entry #text   the whole text, as the editor has reported it so far
    entry #lineCount   the number of lines, counting a trailing unterminated line
    entry #line   the text of a line by its zero-based number, without its terminator, or `Unset`
    entry #apply   the text within a range: `document(range)`
        The end is clamped to be no earlier than the start, so an inverted range gives empty
        text.
    entry #offset   the ordinal offset into the text of a position, clamped to the document
    entry #position   the position of an ordinal offset into the text
    entry #word   the run of letters, digits and underscores under a position, or `Unset`
        The word is found by extending left and right from the position; a position on
        whitespace or punctuation gives `Unset`.
    entry #fullRange   the range from the first position to the end of the text
        What a diagnostic covering the whole document uses.
  entry .Workspace   the session's state as a handler sees it: open documents, the editor's details, the client
    detail
        The handle every handler reads as `workspace`: the URIs of the open documents, a
        snapshot of any one of them, what the editor reported at `initialize` (its process
        id, name and version, root, folders, capabilities and trace setting), and `#client`,
        the channel back to the editor. An exclusive capability, confined to the one
        dispatch like `Document`; the values it returns are pure snapshots.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        object Features:
          import Lsp.*

          def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
            opened:
              val others: List[Text] = workspace.documents.filter(_ != document.uri)
              client.logMessage(t"opened ${document.uri}; also open: ${others.join(t", ")}")
        ```
    refer exegesis.Lsp.Document
    refer exegesis.Lsp.Client
    entry #documents   the URIs of every document the editor has opened and not closed
    entry #document   a snapshot of an open document by URI, as a `TextDocumentItem`, or `Unset`
    entry #processId   the editor's process id, if it reported one
    entry #clientInfo   the editor's name and version, if it reported them
    entry #root   the workspace root URI the editor reported, if any
    entry #folders   the workspace folders the editor reported
    entry #clientCapabilities   the editor's capabilities, as the raw JSON it sent
        Not modelled, since the client-capabilities record is large and a server reads only
        the corners it cares about; a `dynamicAccess.dynamicJson` import reaches into it.
    entry #trace   the trace level the editor last set with `$/setTrace`, if any
    entry #client   the notifications the server may send its editor, as an `Lsp.Client`
        Confined with the workspace: it captures the handle it was read from.
  entry .Focused   the shape of a document-scoped handler with no payload
      `(document: Document^, workspace: Workspace^, emit: Emit[Lsp.Error]^) ?=> result`. The
      handler is lent the document the request names, the workspace, and an emitter which
      `raise(Lsp.Error(…))` reports through; the result is pure, so nothing lent can leave by
      return value, closure or task. `OpenedHandler`, `ClosedHandler`,
      `DocumentSymbolsHandler`, `FoldingRangesHandler`, `DocumentLinksHandler`,
      `CodeLensesHandler`, `DocumentColorsHandler` and `SemanticTokensHandler` are instances.
  entry .Focused1   a document-scoped handler with one tagged payload
      As `Focused`, with a first context parameter `payload: payload` which carries a tag
      (`Position aka "position"`, `Range aka "range"`, …) and is read through the accessor of
      that name. `HoverHandler` is `Focused1[Position aka "position", Optional[Hover]]`.
  entry .Focused2   a document-scoped handler with two tagged payloads
      As `Focused1`, with two payloads; `CompleteHandler` takes a `position` and an optional
      `context`, `RenameHandler` a `position` and a `newName`.
  entry .Focused3   a document-scoped handler with three tagged payloads
      As `Focused1`, with three payloads; `FormatOnTypeHandler` takes a `position`, the
      `character` typed and the formatting `options`.
  entry .Ambient   the shape of a workspace-scoped handler with no payload
      `(workspace: Workspace^, emit: Emit[Lsp.Error]^) ?=> result`: no single document is the
      subject, so only the workspace and the emitter are lent. `ReadyHandler` and
      `TerminatingHandler` are instances.
  entry .Ambient1   a workspace-scoped handler with one tagged payload
      As `Ambient`, with a tagged payload; `WorkspaceSymbolsHandler` reads a `query`,
      `CommandHandler` its `arguments`, `ResolveHandler` its `item`.
  entry .ReadyHandler   the handler for `initialized`: `Ambient[Unit]`
  entry .TerminatingHandler   the handler for `shutdown`: `Ambient[Unit]`
  entry .OpenedHandler   the handler for `textDocument/didOpen`: `Focused[Unit]`
  entry .ChangedHandler   the handler for `textDocument/didChange`, with the `changes` applied
      `Focused1[List[TextDocumentContentChangeEvent] aka "changes", Unit]`; by the time it
      runs the session has already applied the changes, so `document.text` is current.
  entry .SavedHandler   the handler for `textDocument/didSave`, with the `savedText` if the editor sent it
  entry .ClosedHandler   the handler for `textDocument/didClose`: `Focused[Unit]`
      The document is still readable during the handler and removed afterwards.
  entry .SavingHandler   the handler for `textDocument/willSave`, with the `reason`
  entry .SavingEditsHandler   the handler for `textDocument/willSaveWaitUntil`: edits to apply before saving
  entry .HoverHandler   the handler for `textDocument/hover`: a `position` to an optional `Hover`
  entry .CompleteHandler   the handler for `textDocument/completion`: `position` and `context` to a list
  entry .DefinitionHandler   the handler for `textDocument/definition`: a `position` to locations
  entry .ReferencesHandler   the handler for `textDocument/references`, with `includeDeclaration`
  entry .DocumentSymbolsHandler   the handler for `textDocument/documentSymbol`
  entry .FormatHandler   the handler for `textDocument/formatting`, with the editor's `options`
  entry .RenameHandler   the handler for `textDocument/rename`: `position` and `newName` to a `WorkspaceEdit`
  entry .CodeActionsHandler   the handler for `textDocument/codeAction`: a `range` and its `context`
  entry .SignatureHelpHandler   the handler for `textDocument/signatureHelp`
  entry .DeclarationHandler   the handler for `textDocument/declaration`
  entry .TypeDefinitionHandler   the handler for `textDocument/typeDefinition`
  entry .ImplementationHandler   the handler for `textDocument/implementation`
  entry .DocumentHighlightsHandler   the handler for `textDocument/documentHighlight`
  entry .FoldingRangesHandler   the handler for `textDocument/foldingRange`
  entry .SelectionRangesHandler   the handler for `textDocument/selectionRange`: `positions` to ranges
  entry .DocumentLinksHandler   the handler for `textDocument/documentLink`
  entry .CodeLensesHandler   the handler for `textDocument/codeLens`
  entry .DocumentColorsHandler   the handler for `textDocument/documentColor`
  entry .ColorPresentationsHandler   the handler for `textDocument/colorPresentation`: a `color` and its `range`
  entry .FormatRangeHandler   the handler for `textDocument/rangeFormatting`: a `range` and `options`
  entry .FormatOnTypeHandler   the handler for `textDocument/onTypeFormatting`: `position`, `character`, `options`
  entry .PrepareRenameHandler   the handler for `textDocument/prepareRename`: the range to rename, or `Unset`
  entry .CallHierarchyHandler   the prepare handler of `callHierarchy`: a `position` to hierarchy items
  entry .IncomingCallsHandler   the incoming handler of `callHierarchy`: an `item` to its callers
  entry .OutgoingCallsHandler   the outgoing handler of `callHierarchy`: an `item` to its callees
  entry .TypeHierarchyHandler   the prepare handler of `typeHierarchy`: a `position` to hierarchy items
  entry .SupertypesHandler   the supertypes handler of `typeHierarchy`: an `item` to its supertypes
  entry .SubtypesHandler   the subtypes handler of `typeHierarchy`: an `item` to its subtypes
  entry .SemanticTokensHandler   the handler for `textDocument/semanticTokens/full`
  entry .SemanticTokensDeltaHandler   the handler for `textDocument/semanticTokens/full/delta`, with `previousResultId`
  entry .SemanticTokensRangeHandler   the handler for `textDocument/semanticTokens/range`, with the `range`
  entry .InlayHintsHandler   the handler for `textDocument/inlayHint`, with the `range`
  entry .InlineValuesHandler   the handler for `textDocument/inlineValue`: a `range` and its `context`
  entry .LinkedEditingRangeHandler   the handler for `textDocument/linkedEditingRange`
  entry .MonikersHandler   the handler for `textDocument/moniker`
  entry .DiagnosticsHandler   the handler for `textDocument/diagnostic`: `identifier` and `previousResultId`
  entry .WorkspaceSymbolsHandler   the handler for `workspace/symbol`: a `query` to symbols
  entry .CommandHandler   the handler for one `workspace/executeCommand` name: `arguments` to an optional result
  entry .ConfigurationHandler   the handler for `workspace/didChangeConfiguration`, with the `settings`
  entry .WatchedFilesHandler   the handler for `workspace/didChangeWatchedFiles`, with the `changes`
  entry .FoldersChangedHandler   the handler for `workspace/didChangeWorkspaceFolders`, with the `event`
  entry .CreatingFilesHandler   the handler for `workspace/willCreateFiles`: `files` to an optional edit
  entry .CreatedFilesHandler   the handler for `workspace/didCreateFiles`
  entry .RenamingFilesHandler   the handler for `workspace/willRenameFiles`: `files` to an optional edit
  entry .RenamedFilesHandler   the handler for `workspace/didRenameFiles`
  entry .DeletingFilesHandler   the handler for `workspace/willDeleteFiles`: `files` to an optional edit
  entry .DeletedFilesHandler   the handler for `workspace/didDeleteFiles`
  entry .ResolveHandler   the handler for a `*/resolve` request: an `item` returned filled in
      `ResolveHandler[item] = Ambient1[item aka "item", item]`: the editor sends back an item
      it received earlier, and the handler returns it with its lazily-computed fields set.
      Read the item as `item[CompletionItem]` (or whichever type), since the accessor is
      generic.
  entry .document   the current document, inside a document-scoped handler
      A transparent inline accessor for the `Document^` context parameter: `document.text`,
      `document.word(position)`, `document.uri`. Available only where a `Focused` handler's
      parameters are in scope.
  entry .workspace   the workspace handle, inside any handler
      A transparent inline accessor for the `Workspace^` context parameter.
  entry .client   the editor, as an `Lsp.Client`, inside any handler
      `workspace.client`: the channel for `publishDiagnostics`, `showMessage`, `logMessage`
      and the other notifications a server sends unbidden. It captures the workspace handle
      and is confined with it.
  entry .position   the `position` payload of a hover, definition, rename or similar request
      An inline accessor for the `Position aka "position"` context parameter.
  entry .range   the `range` payload of a code-action, inlay-hint, range-formatting or similar request
  entry .options   the `FormattingOptions` payload of a formatting request
  entry .newName   the new name a rename request asks for
  entry .character   the character typed, in an on-type formatting request
  entry .savedText   the text the editor sent with `didSave`, if it included it
  entry .reason   why a document is being saved, in a `saving` or `savingEdits` handler
  entry .color   the `Color` a colour-presentation request concerns
  entry .positions   the positions a selection-range request asks about
  entry .previousResultId   the result id a semantic-tokens delta request builds on
  entry .identifier   the diagnostic identifier a pull-diagnostics request names, if any
  entry .query   the query text of a workspace-symbol request
  entry .arguments   the arguments of a command, as a list of raw JSON values, if any
  entry .settings   the settings a configuration change delivers, as raw JSON
  entry .event   the folders-changed event
  entry .includeDeclaration   whether a references request wants the declaration included
  entry .item   the item a resolve, call-hierarchy or type-hierarchy request concerns
      Generic over the item's type, so the handler names it: `item[CompletionItem]`,
      `item[CallHierarchyItem]`, `item[TypeHierarchyItem]`.
  entry .context   the context of a completion, code-action or inline-value request
      Generic: `Optional[CompletionContext]` in a `complete` handler, `CodeActionContext` in
      `codeActions`, `InlineValueContext` in `inlineValues`; the type is inferred from the
      handler.
  entry .changes   the content changes of a `changed` handler, or the file events of `watchedFiles`
  entry .files   the files a file-operation handler concerns
  entry .ready   registers what runs once the editor sends `initialized`
      Every registration combinator takes a handler and needs an `Lsp.Registry^` in scope —
      the block of `listen`, or a method taking `(using Lsp.Registry^)` — and records the
      handler in it; the combinators are ordinary methods, not inline, so a handler's context
      function is typed once at the definition. A second registration of the same feature
      replaces the first. `ready` is a `ReadyHandler`, run after the handshake, where a server
      starts indexing or asks the editor for configuration.
  entry .terminating   registers what runs when the editor sends `shutdown`
      A `TerminatingHandler`; the `exit` notification which follows ends the process's input
      and so the `listen` loop.
  entry .opened   registers what runs when the editor opens a document
      An `OpenedHandler`: the document is in the store before it runs, so publishing
      diagnostics for it is the usual body.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object Features:
        import Lsp.*

        def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
          opened:
            client.publishDiagnostics
              ( document.uri,
                List
                  ( Diagnostic
                      ( range    = document.fullRange,
                        severity = DiagnosticSeverity.Warning,
                        message  = t"a diagnostic for the whole document" ) ) )
      ```
  entry .changed   registers what runs after the editor's changes to a document are applied
      A `ChangedHandler`; `changes` is the batch the editor sent and `document.text` already
      reflects it.
  entry .saved   registers what runs when the editor saves a document
      A `SavedHandler`; `savedText` is the saved content if the editor includes it.
  entry .closed   registers what runs when the editor closes a document
      A `ClosedHandler`; the document is removed from the store after it runs.
  entry .saving   registers what runs before a document is saved
      A `SavingHandler`, given the `reason`.
  entry .savingEdits   registers the edits applied before a document is saved
      A `SavingEditsHandler`: `textDocument/willSaveWaitUntil`, which the editor awaits.
  entry .hover   registers the hover handler, and advertises `hoverProvider`
      A `HoverHandler`: the `position` to an `Optional[Hover]`, `Unset` meaning nothing to
      show.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object Features:
        import Lsp.*

        def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
          hover:
            document.word(position).let: word =>
              Hover(MarkupContent(value = t"**$word**"))
      ```
  entry .complete   registers the completion handler, with its trigger characters
      `complete(triggers*)(handler)`: the trigger characters become the advertised
      `CompletionOptions`, and `resolveProvider` is set if `resolveCompletion` is registered.
      The handler is a `CompleteHandler`, reading `position` and the optional `context`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object Features:
        import Lsp.*

        def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
          complete(t"."):
            CompletionList(items = List(CompletionItem(label = t"length")))
      ```
  entry .definition   registers the go-to-definition handler
      A `DefinitionHandler`: the `position` to a list of `Location`s, empty for none.
  entry .references   registers the find-references handler
      A `ReferencesHandler`, reading `position` and `includeDeclaration`.
  entry .documentSymbols   registers the document-symbols handler
  entry .format   registers the whole-document formatting handler
      A `FormatHandler`, reading the editor's `options`, returning the edits to apply.
  entry .rename   registers the rename handler
      A `RenameHandler`, reading `position` and `newName`, returning a `WorkspaceEdit`.
  entry .codeActions   registers the code-actions handler
      A `CodeActionsHandler`, reading the `range` and the `context` holding its diagnostics.
  entry .signatureHelp   registers the signature-help handler, with its trigger characters
      `signatureHelp(triggers*)(handler)`: the triggers become the advertised
      `SignatureHelpOptions`.
  entry .declaration   registers the go-to-declaration handler
  entry .typeDefinition   registers the go-to-type-definition handler
  entry .implementation   registers the go-to-implementation handler
  entry .documentHighlights   registers the document-highlights handler
  entry .foldingRanges   registers the folding-ranges handler
  entry .selectionRanges   registers the selection-ranges handler
      A `SelectionRangesHandler`: one `SelectionRange` chain per position in `positions`.
  entry .documentLinks   registers the document-links handler
  entry .codeLenses   registers the code-lens handler
      `resolveProvider` in the advertised `CodeLensOptions` is set if `resolveCodeLens` is
      registered.
  entry .documentColors   registers the document-colours handler, and advertises `colorProvider`
  entry .colorPresentations   registers the colour-presentations handler
  entry .formatRange   registers the range-formatting handler
  entry .formatOnType   registers the on-type formatting handler, with its trigger characters
      `formatOnType(first, more*)(handler)`: the protocol requires one trigger character; the
      handler reads `position`, the `character` typed and the `options`.
  entry .prepareRename   registers the handler which says what a rename at a position would cover
  entry .callHierarchy   registers the three call-hierarchy handlers together
      `callHierarchy(prepare)(incoming, outgoing)`: the capability is atomic, so the prepare
      handler (a `position` to items), the incoming-calls handler and the outgoing-calls
      handler (each an `item` to calls) are registered at once.
  entry .typeHierarchy   registers the three type-hierarchy handlers together
      `typeHierarchy(prepare)(supertypes, subtypes)`, as `callHierarchy`.
  entry .semanticTokens   registers the full semantic-tokens handler, with its legend
      `semanticTokens(legend)(handler)`: the `SemanticTokensLegend` is required, since the
      token data indexes into it, and is advertised in `SemanticTokensOptions`.
  entry .semanticTokensDelta   registers the semantic-tokens delta handler
      A `SemanticTokensDeltaHandler`, reading `previousResultId`.
  entry .semanticTokensRange   registers the ranged semantic-tokens handler
      Sets `range` in the advertised `SemanticTokensOptions`, which exist only if
      `semanticTokens` was registered too.
  entry .inlayHints   registers the inlay-hints handler
  entry .inlineValues   registers the inline-values handler
  entry .linkedEditingRange   registers the linked-editing-range handler
  entry .monikers   registers the moniker handler
  entry .diagnostics   registers the pull-diagnostics handler, with its options
      `diagnostics(options)(handler)`: the `DiagnosticOptions` are required by the protocol
      and advertised as given.
  entry .workspaceSymbols   registers the workspace-symbol search handler
      A `WorkspaceSymbolsHandler`, reading `query`.
  entry .command   registers a command by name
      `command(name)(handler)`: `workspace/executeCommand` requests are multiplexed across
      the registered names, which are advertised in `ExecuteCommandOptions`. The handler is a
      `CommandHandler`, reading `arguments` (an optional list of raw JSON) and returning an
      optional JSON result. A request for a name nobody registered is answered with an
      `InvalidParams` error, _the command `name` is not registered_.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object Features:
        import Lsp.*

        def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
          command(t"demo.reverse"):
            arguments.let(_.head).let: argument =>
              val text: Text = argument.as[Text]
              Text(StringBuilder(text.s).reverse.toString).in[Json]
      ```
  entry .configuration   registers what runs when the editor's configuration changes
      A `ConfigurationHandler`, reading `settings` as raw JSON.
  entry .watchedFiles   registers what runs when a watched file changes
      A `WatchedFilesHandler`, reading `changes`, a list of `FileEvent`.
  entry .foldersChanged   registers what runs when workspace folders are added or removed
  entry .creatingFiles   registers what runs before files are created, returning an optional edit
  entry .createdFiles   registers what runs after files are created
  entry .renamingFiles   registers what runs before files are renamed, returning an optional edit
  entry .renamedFiles   registers what runs after files are renamed
  entry .deletingFiles   registers what runs before files are deleted, returning an optional edit
  entry .deletedFiles   registers what runs after files are deleted
  entry .resolveCompletion   registers the handler which fills in a completion item on demand
      A `ResolveHandler[CompletionItem]`, read as `item[CompletionItem]`; registering it sets
      `resolveProvider` in the advertised `CompletionOptions`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      object Features:
        import Lsp.*

        def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
          resolveCompletion(item[CompletionItem].copy(detail = t"resolved"))
      ```
  entry .resolveCodeAction   registers the handler which fills in a code action on demand
  entry .resolveCodeLens   registers the handler which fills in a code lens on demand
      Sets `resolveProvider` in the advertised `CodeLensOptions`.
  entry .resolveDocumentLink   registers the handler which fills in a document link on demand
      Sets `resolveProvider` in the advertised `DocumentLinkOptions`.
  entry .resolveInlayHint   registers the handler which fills in an inlay hint on demand
  entry .resolveWorkspaceSymbol   registers the handler which fills in a workspace symbol on demand
  entry .capabilities   registers an adjustment to the derived capabilities, applied last
      `capabilities(adjust)`: an escape hatch for a corner the derivation does not cover. The
      function is pure (`ServerCapabilities -> ServerCapabilities`) and runs once, at
      `initialize`, on the record `Registry#capabilities` derived.
  entry .dispatcher   the JSON-RPC dispatcher for any implementation of the `Lsp` trait
      `Lsp.Dispatch(server)`: a `Json => Optional[Json]` which routes each message to the
      wire method of `server` its method names and returns the response, or `Unset` for a
      notification. The low-level escape hatch for a server written as a class implementing
      `Lsp` by hand; `listen`'s own session is dispatched the same way.
  entry .Observer   a hook on the raw traffic: every message as it crosses the transport
    detail
        A server's standard output is the wire, so it cannot print a log of what it exchanges;
        an observer sees each message instead, as the JSON body it was carried as — inbound
        before parsing, so a message which fails to decode is observed too, and outbound
        before framing, so both directions read alike. `listen`, `proxy` and `LspSessional`
        take one. Both methods are abstract, and the observer is called from the writer task
        and the reader task at once, so an implementation must be safe to call from two
        threads.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val observer = new Lsp.Observer:
          def received(message: Text): Unit = Err.println(t"recv $message")
          def sent(message: Text): Unit = Err.println(t"send $message")
        ```

        `Observer.Silent` is the default, and `Observer.silent` supplies it contextually to a
        session.
    entry .Silent   the observer which does nothing, as a pure value
    entry .silent   the given which supplies `Silent` to an `LspSessional`; in implicit scope
    entry #received   called with each message read from the wire, before it is parsed
    entry #sent   called with each message written, as its encoded body, before it is framed
  entry .Listener   what a client does with the messages a server sends unbidden
    detail
        The inbound half of a client's exchange: the notifications a server sends without
        being asked (diagnostics, window and log messages, traces, telemetry, progress,
        cancellations), and the requests it makes of its client. Every method has a no-op
        default, so a listener implements only what it acts on, and `Listener.Quiet` is the
        default, supplied by the given `Listener.quiet`. A listener is bound as a local given
        and found by `Lsp.Server.sessional` when a session opens.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        given diagnostics: Lsp.Listener = new Lsp.Listener:
          override def diagnostics(uri: Text, version: Optional[Int], reports: List[Lsp.Diagnostic])
          :   Unit =
            reports.each { report => Err.println(t"$uri: ${report.message}") }
        ```

        The listener runs on the session's reader task, so a method must not await a request
        of the same connection: the answer would arrive on the thread it is blocking.
    entry .Quiet   the listener which ignores everything
    entry .quiet   the given which supplies `Quiet`; in implicit scope
    entry #diagnostics   diagnostics the server published for a document, with its version if given
    entry #message   a `window/showMessage`, to show the user, with its level
    entry #log   a `window/logMessage`, for the editor's log, with its level
    entry #trace   a `$/logTrace` message
    entry #telemetry   a `telemetry/event`, as raw JSON
    entry #progress   a `$/progress` notification: its token and value, as raw JSON
    entry #cancel   a `$/cancelRequest`, with the id the server wants cancelled
    entry #request   a request the server made of its client, answered raw
        `workspace/applyEdit`, `workspace/configuration`, `window/showMessageRequest`,
        `client/registerCapability` and whatever else a server asks: the set is open, so the
        method and parameters are raw and the answer is raw JSON. `Unset`, the default,
        answers with JSON `null`, which a server must tolerate for a capability its client
        never claimed.
    entry #intercept   sees every message first; `true` means it is handled and routed no further
        What a proxy uses to relay messages, responses included, rather than act on them; a
        normal client leaves the default `false`.
  entry .Transit   what a proxy does with a message a hook was shown
    detail
        The result of a `rewrite.outbound` or `rewrite.inbound` hook. `Forward` passes the
        message on unchanged, which is also what happens to everything no hook sees;
        `Rewrite(message)` passes on another message instead; `Drop` swallows it — a request
        dropped on the way out is never answered, so drop notifications, or answer the request
        instead; and `Answer(result)` answers an outbound request here, so the server never
        sees it, and is ignored for a message which is not a request and for an inbound
        message.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        rewrite.outbound: (method, message) =>
          if method == t"textDocument/inlineValue" then Lsp.Transit.Drop else Lsp.Transit.Forward
        ```
  entry .Server   a language server this process can speak to: a command, or a pair of streams
    detail
        A plain value describing where a server is, holding no channel of its own:
        `Server.Process(command)` is a server launched as a subprocess, whose standard input
        and output carry the protocol, and `Server.Streams(input, output)` one already running
        behind a pair of `java.io` streams — in this process, behind a socket, or a fixture in
        a test. `Server(command)` and `Server.streams(input, output)` build each, widened to
        `Server`.

        A session with it is opened by `session`, aperture's `Sessional` extension, through
        the given `.sessional`: the channel is minted when the session opens, an
        `Lsp.Connection` is lent for the duration of the block, and both are disposed of
        afterwards, so the connection cannot outlive the server. A subprocess is killed when
        the session ends, rather than waited for.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import threads.virtualThreads
        import probates.awaitProbate
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment

        supervise:
          Lsp.Server(sh"rust-analyzer").session: server ?=>
            server.initialize(root = t"file:///project")
            server.initialized()
            server.shutdown()
            server.exit()
        ```
    refer exegesis.Lsp.Connection
    refer exegesis.LspSessional
    entry .apply   a server launched as a subprocess: `Lsp.Server(command)`
    entry .streams   a server already running behind an input and an output stream
        Which stream is which is from this process's point of view: `input` is what the server
        writes, `output` what it reads.
    entry .sessional   the given which opens a session with a `Server`; in implicit scope
        An `LspSessional`, built from the context: it needs a `Monitor`, a `Probate`,
        `Diagnostics`, a `WorkingDirectory` and an `Environment` (for launching a
        subprocess), a `Tactic[Lsp.Error]`, and a `Listener^` and `Observer^` — the quiet
        and silent ones unless a given of one's own is in scope. A listener or observer is
        supplied by binding it as a given, never by passing it: an instance capturing the
        session's monitor could not be bound to a name at the use site.
  entry .proxy   serves an editor over stdio while forwarding everything to a server upstream
    detail
        `Lsp.proxy(upstream, observer)(register)`: this process reads an editor from its
        standard input and writes to its standard output, holds a session with `upstream`,
        and relays every message between them, amending what the block registers on the
        lent `Lsp.Proxy` through the `rewrite` combinators. The relay is asynchronous in both
        directions, so requests are in flight in any number and answered in any order,
        and request ids pass through unchanged. Everything unregistered — methods this
        library does not model, capabilities it does not know — crosses byte for byte.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        Lsp.proxy(Lsp.Server(sh"rust-analyzer")):
          rewrite.capabilities(_.copy(hoverProvider = true))

          rewrite.hover: hover =>
            hover.copy(contents = Lsp.MarkupContent(value = t"_(proxied)_ ${hover.contents.value}"))

          rewrite.diagnostics(_.filter(_.severity != Lsp.DiagnosticSeverity.Hint))
        ```

        It needs a `Stdio^`, a `Monitor^`, a `Probate`, a `WorkingDirectory`, an
        `Environment`, `Diagnostics`, and tactics for `Lsp.Error` (a server which fails to
        launch is `Reason.Unstarted`), `Framing.Error` (a malformed frame) and
        `Truncation.Error` (a stream cut short). The registration block runs once,
        synchronously, before the session opens, and may capture the monitor. `Lsp.Proxy.run`
        is the same call with an explicit observer.
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Proxy
  refer exegesis.rewrite
  refer exegesis.upstream
  entry .listen   serves a language server over standard input and output, from registered handlers
    detail
        `Lsp.listen(name, version, observer)(register)`: the block registers the server's
        handlers on a lent `Lsp.Registry`; once it returns the registry is consumed, the
        `ServerCapabilities` are derived from it, and the loop reads `Content-Length`-framed
        JSON-RPC from standard input until it is exhausted, dispatching each message to its
        handler on the reading thread. A single writer task drains the outgoing channel —
        responses and the notifications handlers send through `client` alike — and frames each
        onto standard output, so writes never interleave. `name` and `version` are what the
        `initialize` result reports as `ServerInfo`; the observer, if given, sees every message
        in both directions.

        A server grows by registration, and its capabilities grow with it:

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label A server with no features answers initialize, and tracks documents
          code
              import soundness.*

              object Features:
                import Lsp.*

                def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit = ()
        frame
          label Registering hover advertises hoverProvider
          code
              import soundness.*

              object Features:
                import Lsp.*

                def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
                  hover:
                    document.word(position).let: word =>
                      Hover(MarkupContent(value = t"**$word**"))
        frame
          label Registering completion advertises completionProvider, with its trigger
          code
              import soundness.*

              object Features:
                import Lsp.*

                def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
                  hover:
                    document.word(position).let: word =>
                      Hover(MarkupContent(value = t"**$word**"))

                  complete(t"."):
                    CompletionList(items = List(CompletionItem(label = t"length")))
        frame
          label An opened handler publishes diagnostics as each document arrives
          code
              import soundness.*

              object Features:
                import Lsp.*

                def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
                  hover:
                    document.word(position).let: word =>
                      Hover(MarkupContent(value = t"**$word**"))

                  complete(t"."):
                    CompletionList(items = List(CompletionItem(label = t"length")))

                  opened:
                    client.publishDiagnostics
                      ( document.uri,
                        List
                          ( Diagnostic
                              ( range    = document.fullRange,
                                severity = DiagnosticSeverity.Warning,
                                message  = t"a diagnostic for the whole document" ) ) )
        ##
        ```

        The whole server is then a `main` in the resident-daemon idiom, so that an editor
        which restarts its language server often reconnects to a JVM already running:

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        object DemoServer:
          def main(args: Array[Text]): Unit = cli:
            execute:
              supervise:
                Lsp.listen(t"Demo", t"0.1.0"):
                  Features.register
              Exit.Ok
        ```

        It needs a `Stdio`, a `Monitor` and a `Probate` (the writer is a task), and tactics
        for `Framing.Error` and `Truncation.Error`. Faults are answered, not thrown: a message
        which is not JSON gets a `-32700` parse error, a payload which does not decode a
        `-32602` invalid-params error, any other exception in a handler a `-32603` internal
        error, and an `Lsp.Error` a handler raises an error response with the reason's wire
        code and the request's id — or, for a notification, a `window/logMessage`. The
        registry, the session and the dispatcher are all locals of the call: nothing holding
        a capability is stored in an application-lifetime object, and capture checking
        rejects a handler which tries (see `Document` and `Workspace`).
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Registry
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Document
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Workspace
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Client
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Error
  entry .Client   the notifications a server sends its editor
    detail
        The server-to-client half of the protocol, as the server calls it: a handler reaches
        one as `client` and sends `publishDiagnostics`, `showMessage`, `logMessage`,
        `telemetry`, `logTrace`, `progress` or `cancelRequest`. Only notifications are
        exposed, never requests: a handler runs on the thread which reads the editor's
        messages, so a round-trip awaited from inside one would wait for a response that
        thread must read, and deadlock. A client which needs the editor's answer to something
        asks in `ready` and reads the result when the editor's notification arrives.

        The wire methods — `` `textDocument/publishDiagnostics` ``, `` `window/showMessage` ``,
        `` `window/logMessage` ``, `` `telemetry/event` ``, `` `$/logTrace` ``,
        `` `$/progress` `` and `` `$/cancelRequest` `` — are the `@rpc` surface the generated
        proxy implements; the named methods wrap them with the usual defaults (no version,
        `Info` and `Log` levels, no verbose trace). A client that *receives* these messages —
        `LspSessional`'s reader — dispatches the same trait to an `Lsp.Listener`.

        A `Client` is a shared capability: publishing from a task spawned in a handler is
        legitimate, since the outgoing channel is multi-producer, but the handle is confined
        to the serving scope which minted it and cannot be stashed for use after the session
        ends.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        object Features:
          import Lsp.*

          def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
            saved:
              client.showMessage(t"saved ${document.uri}")
              client.logMessage(t"version ${document.version}")
        ```
    refer exegesis.Lsp.Workspace
    refer exegesis.Lsp.Listener
    entry #publishDiagnostics   sends the diagnostics for a document, replacing those shown
        An empty list clears them. The document's version is not sent.
    entry #showMessage   shows the user a message at `Info` level
    entry #logMessage   writes a line to the editor's log at `Log` level
    entry #telemetry   sends a `telemetry/event` with a raw JSON payload
    entry #logTrace   sends a `$/logTrace` message, without the verbose part
    entry #progress   sends a `$/progress` notification: a token and a value, both raw JSON
        A progress token is a string or an integer and a value is protocol-specific, so both
        are raw.
    entry #cancelRequest   asks the editor to cancel a request it made, by id
  entry .Connection   the client's handle on a running server, lent for the duration of a session
    detail
        The mirror of a server's session: what `session` lends an editor, or a proxy, while
        it is open. Every request the protocol defines has a method — `#hover`, `#complete`,
        `#definition`, `#rename`, … — taking the document's URI and the request's parameters
        and returning the same types a server's handlers return, and every notification
        (`#open`, `#edit`, `#save`, `#close`, `#initialized`, `#exit`, `#configure`) returns
        nothing. A request blocks the caller until its response arrives, but never blocks
        the reader task, so several may be in flight at once from different tasks and may be
        answered out of order.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import threads.virtualThreads
        import probates.awaitProbate
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment

        def labels(source: Text): List[Text] = supervise:
          Lsp.Server(sh"rust-analyzer").session: server ?=>
            server.initialize(root = t"file:///project")
            server.initialized()
            server.open(t"file:///project/src/main.rs", t"rust", source)
            server.complete(t"file:///project/src/main.rs", Lsp.Position(0, 0)).items.map(_.label)
        ```

        Every request needs a `Tactic[Lsp.Error]`: an error response from the server is
        raised as an `Lsp.Error` whose reason is the one the wire code names
        (`Lsp.Error.reason`), or `Internal` with the server's own message as the details for
        a code outside the standard set, rather than being awaited forever. The connection
        is a shared capability, driven by its writer, its reader and the caller at once, and
        a `JsonRpc`, so `#send` puts a raw message on its channel.

        The generated proxies behind the methods are the public `vals` `#lifecycle`,
        `#language`, `#navigation`, `#editing`, `#advanced`, `#workspace` and `#resolve`, one
        per wire trait, for a request the named methods do not cover (selection ranges,
        colours, the hierarchies, resolves, file operations): each wire method takes the
        protocol's own parameters and summons its result's decoder under the call's tactic.
    refer exegesis.Lsp.Server
    refer exegesis.Lsp.Error
    refer exegesis.Lsp.Listener
    entry #Origin   the far end of the connection: `Lsp`
    entry #lifecycle   the generated client for `LspLifecycle`: initialize, shutdown and document sync
    entry #language   the generated client for `LspLanguage`: completion, hover, definition and the rest
    entry #navigation   the generated client for `LspNavigation`: declaration, folding, links, colours
    entry #editing   the generated client for `LspEditing`: range and on-type formatting, hierarchies
    entry #advanced   the generated client for `LspAdvanced`: semantic tokens, inlay hints, diagnostics
    entry #workspace   the generated client for `LspWorkspace`: symbols, commands, file operations
    entry #resolve   the generated client for `LspResolve`: the `*/resolve` requests
    entry #send   sends a message exactly as given, minting no id and awaiting no answer
        The raw seam a proxy forwards through, so the editor's own ids and the methods this
        library does not model cross unchanged.
    entry #initialize   the handshake: sends `initialize` and returns the server's capabilities
        `initialize(root, name, version, folders, capabilities)`: all optional; the client
        reports itself as `soundness` with no version and no capabilities unless told
        otherwise, and the server's answer is an `InitializeResult`. Follow it with
        `#initialized`.
    entry #initialized   tells the server the handshake is complete
    entry #shutdown   asks the server to shut down, and awaits its acknowledgement
    entry #exit   tells the server to exit; the notification which ends a well-behaved server
    entry #open   tells the server a document is open, with its language, text and version
        `open(uri, language, text, version = 1)`.
    entry #edit   sends a batch of changes to an open document, with its new version
    entry #replace   the whole-document form of `#edit`: replaces the text under a new version
    entry #save   tells the server a document was saved, with its text if the server wants it
    entry #close   tells the server a document is closed
    entry #hover   the hover at a position, or `Unset`
    entry #complete   the completions at a position, with an optional `CompletionContext`
    entry #definition   the definition locations of the symbol at a position
    entry #references   the references to the symbol at a position, including its declaration by default
    entry #symbols   the document's symbols
    entry #format   the edits which format a document under the given options
    entry #rename   the workspace edit which renames the symbol at a position
    entry #codeActions   the code actions at a range, given its context
    entry #signatureHelp   the signature help at a position, or `Unset`
    entry #declaration   the declaration locations of the symbol at a position
    entry #typeDefinition   the type-definition locations of the symbol at a position
    entry #implementation   the implementation locations of the symbol at a position
    entry #highlights   the document highlights for the symbol at a position
    entry #foldingRanges   the document's folding ranges
    entry #documentLinks   the document's links
    entry #codeLenses   the document's code lenses
    entry #semanticTokens   the document's full semantic tokens
    entry #inlayHints   the inlay hints within a range
    entry #diagnostics   the document's pull diagnostics, as a full report
    entry #search   the workspace symbols matching a query
    entry #execute   runs a command by name with raw JSON arguments, returning its raw result
    entry #configure   tells the server the configuration changed, with the settings as raw JSON
  entry .Dispatch   builds the JSON-RPC dispatcher for an `Lsp` implementation
    detail
        `Lsp.Dispatch(server)` is a `Json => Optional[Json]`: it reads the method a message
        names and routes the message to the generated dispatcher of the wire trait which
        declares it, returning the response or `Unset` for a notification; a message with no
        method is a response and is handled by the lifecycle dispatcher; an unknown method
        gives `Unset`. The dispatch is generated per sub-trait rather than once for the whole
        protocol because obligatory's `JsonRpc.serve` inlines a codec for every method, and
        one class for all of LSP would exceed the JVM's constant-pool limit — which is also
        why the trait is split seven ways. `Lsp.dispatcher` is the same call.
    entry .apply   the dispatcher for a server, routing each message by the trait which declares its method
  entry .Error   the fault a handler raises, and the error response a server answers with
    detail
        Error 147: _the LSP operation failed because `reason`_, numbered by reason (147.1 for
        `Parse` through 147.11 for `Unstarted`). Each `Reason` carries the JSON-RPC wire code
        the protocol assigns it: `Parse` −32700, `InvalidRequest` −32600, `MethodNotFound`
        −32601, `InvalidParams` −32602, `Internal` −32603, `ServerNotInitialized` −32002,
        `RequestCancelled` −32800, `ContentModified` −32801, `ServerCancelled` −32802,
        `RequestFailed` −32803 and `Unstarted` −32000, this library's own, for a server
        subprocess which failed to launch.

        A handler raises one through the `Emit[Lsp.Error]` it is lent, and `Lsp.listen`
        answers the request with an error response carrying the reason's code and
        `#response` as the message — or, for a notification, a `window/logMessage`. The
        session raises `InvalidParams` itself for a request about a document which is not
        open and for a command nobody registered.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        object Features:
          import Lsp.*

          def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
            command(t"demo.run"):
              raise(Lsp.Error(Lsp.Error.Reason.RequestFailed, t"nothing to run"))
              Unset
        ```

        On the client side, `Connection`'s requests raise one for an error response, with the
        reason `.reason` recovers from the wire code, and `LspSessional` raises `Unstarted`
        when the server's command cannot be forked. Constructing one needs `Diagnostics` in
        scope, as every fulminate error does.
    entry .Reason   why the operation failed, each with its JSON-RPC wire `code` and its `number`
        The eleven reasons above. `code` is the wire code, `number` the sub-number of error
        147.
    entry .reason   the reason a wire code names, or `Unset` for a code outside the standard set
        The inverse of `Reason#code`, for a client reading an error response; a server may
        answer with an implementation-defined code, which `Connection` reports as `Internal`.
    entry .communicable   renders a reason as its message, _the request failed_ and the like
    entry #reason   why the operation failed
    entry #details   the message given when the error was raised, if any
    entry #response   the message sent in the error response: the details, or the reason's description
  entry .Registry   where a server's handlers are recorded, lent to the block given to `listen`
    detail
        The target of the registration combinators: `Lsp.listen` mints one, lends it to the
        registration block as a context parameter, and consumes it when the block returns, so
        registration after serving begins is impossible by construction. It is a shared
        capability which cannot escape the block — capture checking rejects a `listen` block
        which assigns `summon[Lsp.Registry]` to an outer variable — and `#capabilities`
        derives the `ServerCapabilities` the server advertises from what was registered.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        object Features:
          import Lsp.*

          def navigation(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
            definition(Nil)
            references(Nil)

          def register(using Lsp.Registry^): Unit =
            navigation
            hover(Unset)
        ```

        Its slots — one public `var` per feature, named for the combinator with a trailing
        `0`, plus the options registered beside a handler — are public only because the
        combinators assign them, and each handler slot is typed `AnyRef | Null`: a union
        mentioning a context-function type would freshen its capture sets at every
        adaptation, so the typed boundary is the combinator's parameter, where a handler
        closing over the registry is rejected, and the session restores the type when it
        dispatches. Read a slot through its combinator, not directly.
    refer exegesis.Lsp.listen
    refer exegesis.Lsp.ServerCapabilities
    entry .Slot   the box a registered handler is kept in
      detail
          A context-function value adapts to any expected type which is not a context
          function by being applied, so a handler cannot inhabit an `Optional` or an `AnyRef`
          slot directly: `Slot[handler](value)` holds it intact, and the session unboxes it.
      entry #value   the handler
    entry #capabilities   the `ServerCapabilities` the registrations imply, with the adjustment applied last
        Each provider flag is set exactly when its handler was registered; the options given
        at registration (completion and signature-help triggers, on-type trigger characters,
        the semantic-tokens legend, diagnostic options, command names) fill in the structured
        providers; `textDocumentSync` is always `Incremental`; and `capabilities(adjust)`, if
        registered, runs on the result.
    entry #ready0   the `ready` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #terminating0   the `terminating` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #opened0   the `opened` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #changed0   the `changed` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #saved0   the `saved` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #closed0   the `closed` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #saving0   the `saving` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #savingEdits0   the `savingEdits` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #hover0   the `hover` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #complete0   the `complete` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #completeTriggers0   the trigger characters `complete` was registered with
    entry #definition0   the `definition` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #references0   the `references` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #documentSymbols0   the `documentSymbols` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #format0   the `format` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #rename0   the `rename` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #codeActions0   the `codeActions` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #signatureHelp0   the `signatureHelp` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #signatureHelpTriggers0   the trigger characters `signatureHelp` was registered with
    entry #declaration0   the `declaration` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #typeDefinition0   the `typeDefinition` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #implementation0   the `implementation` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #documentHighlights0   the `documentHighlights` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #foldingRanges0   the `foldingRanges` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #selectionRanges0   the `selectionRanges` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #documentLinks0   the `documentLinks` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #codeLenses0   the `codeLenses` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #documentColors0   the `documentColors` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #colorPresentations0   the `colorPresentations` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #formatRange0   the `formatRange` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #formatOnType0   the `formatOnType` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #formatOnTypeFirst0   the first trigger character `formatOnType` was registered with
    entry #formatOnTypeMore0   the further trigger characters `formatOnType` was registered with
    entry #prepareRename0   the `prepareRename` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #prepareCallHierarchy0   the prepare handler of `callHierarchy`, boxed, or `null`
    entry #incomingCalls0   the incoming-calls handler of `callHierarchy`, boxed, or `null`
    entry #outgoingCalls0   the outgoing-calls handler of `callHierarchy`, boxed, or `null`
    entry #prepareTypeHierarchy0   the prepare handler of `typeHierarchy`, boxed, or `null`
    entry #supertypes0   the supertypes handler of `typeHierarchy`, boxed, or `null`
    entry #subtypes0   the subtypes handler of `typeHierarchy`, boxed, or `null`
    entry #semanticTokens0   the `semanticTokens` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #semanticTokensLegend0   the legend `semanticTokens` was registered with
    entry #semanticTokensDelta0   the `semanticTokensDelta` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #semanticTokensRange0   the `semanticTokensRange` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #inlayHints0   the `inlayHints` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #inlineValues0   the `inlineValues` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #linkedEditingRange0   the `linkedEditingRange` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #monikers0   the `monikers` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #diagnostics0   the `diagnostics` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #diagnosticOptions0   the options `diagnostics` was registered with
    entry #workspaceSymbols0   the `workspaceSymbols` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #commands0   the registered commands: each name with its boxed handler
    entry #commandNames0   the registered command names, kept apart for the capabilities
    entry #configuration0   the `configuration` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #watchedFiles0   the `watchedFiles` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #foldersChanged0   the `foldersChanged` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #creatingFiles0   the `creatingFiles` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #createdFiles0   the `createdFiles` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #renamingFiles0   the `renamingFiles` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #renamedFiles0   the `renamedFiles` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #deletingFiles0   the `deletingFiles` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #deletedFiles0   the `deletedFiles` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #resolveCompletion0   the `resolveCompletion` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #resolveCodeAction0   the `resolveCodeAction` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #resolveCodeLens0   the `resolveCodeLens` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #resolveDocumentLink0   the `resolveDocumentLink` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #resolveInlayHint0   the `resolveInlayHint` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #resolveWorkspaceSymbol0   the `resolveWorkspaceSymbol` handler, boxed, or `null`
    entry #adjust0   the pure capabilities adjustment `capabilities(adjust)` registered, if any
  entry .Proxy   where a proxy's rewriters and hooks are recorded, lent to the block given to `Lsp.proxy`
    detail
        The target of the `rewrite` combinators, as `Lsp.Registry` is of the server's. Two
        layers of interception live here: the typed rewriters (`rewrite.hover`,
        `rewrite.capabilities`, `rewrite.diagnostics`, …) are keyed by method and act on a
        decoded payload, and the raw hooks (`rewrite.outbound`, `rewrite.inbound`) act on
        whole messages, including methods this library does not model. What neither covers
        is forwarded byte for byte. An exclusive capability, confined to the registration
        block; its public slots are assigned by the combinators and read once, by `Proxy.run`,
        when registration is over.

        The session with the server upstream comes in with the proxy and is reached through
        `upstream`: a rewriter or hook is registered before the session exists but runs while
        it does, so a proxy may ask the server something the editor never asked for.
    refer exegesis.rewrite
    refer exegesis.upstream
    refer exegesis.Lsp.proxy
    entry .Rewriter   a rewriter of one payload, keyed by method in the proxy's tables
      detail
          What `rewrite.hover` and the other typed combinators construct: `rewrite(json)`
          takes a `Tactic[Json.Error]` at each call, from the loop which applies it, so the
          instance the proxy retains holds no tactic of its own, and a payload it cannot
          decode passes through unchanged — a `null` hover, for instance, is forwarded as the
          `null` it is.
      entry #rewrite   the amended payload, or a `Json.Error` raised through the given tactic
    entry .run   runs a proxy with an explicit observer: what `Lsp.proxy` calls
        `Proxy.run(upstream, observer)(register)`, with the same contextual requirements as
        `Lsp.proxy`. The block runs once; then the rules are read out of the proxy, the
        session with `upstream` opens, `rewrite.connected`'s block starts on a task of its
        own, and the loop reads the editor from standard input until it is exhausted. The
        editor's messages are classified by method and forwarded under their own ids; the
        server's are intercepted by a listener which retypes each response by the method of
        the request it answers, applies the matching rewriter, and writes the result to
        standard output. A response to a request the proxy made itself (whose id is a string
        obligatory minted) is routed to the caller awaiting it instead, so it never reaches
        the editor.
    entry #results   the rewriters for responses, by the method of the request they answer
    entry #notices   the rewriters for notifications and server-to-client requests, by method
    entry #outbound0   the `rewrite.outbound` hook, boxed, or `null`
    entry #inbound0   the `rewrite.inbound` hook, boxed, or `null`
    entry #connected0   the `rewrite.connected` block, boxed, or `null`

entry .LspLifecycle   the wire trait for the lifecycle and document-sync methods
    One of the seven `@rpc` traits which, unioned in `Lsp`, make up the wire surface; split
    out so that obligatory's `JsonRpc.serve` and `JsonRpc.proxy` generate a class of
    manageable size per trait. It declares `initialize`, `initialized`, `shutdown`, `exit`,
    `textDocument/didOpen`, `textDocument/didChange`, `textDocument/didSave` and
    `textDocument/didClose`, each taking the protocol's own parameter names and types
    (`textDocument: Lsp.TextDocumentIdentifier`, and so on). `Lsp.Connection#lifecycle` is
    the generated client for it, and `Lsp.listen`'s session implements it. Not exported to
    the `soundness` umbrella: reached with `import exegesis.*`, and needed only by a server
    written as an `Lsp` by hand or a client calling a wire method directly.

entry .LspLanguage   the wire trait for the core language features
    `textDocument/completion`, `textDocument/hover`, `textDocument/definition`,
    `textDocument/references`, `textDocument/documentSymbol`, `textDocument/formatting`,
    `textDocument/rename`, `textDocument/codeAction` and `textDocument/signatureHelp`. See
    `LspLifecycle` for why the surface is split; `Lsp.Connection#language` is its client.

entry .LspNavigation   the wire trait for navigation, folding, links, lenses and colours
    `textDocument/declaration`, `textDocument/typeDefinition`,
    `textDocument/implementation`, `textDocument/documentHighlight`,
    `textDocument/foldingRange`, `textDocument/selectionRange`, `textDocument/documentLink`,
    `textDocument/codeLens`, `textDocument/documentColor` and
    `textDocument/colorPresentation`. `Lsp.Connection#navigation` is its client.

entry .LspEditing   the wire trait for range and on-type formatting, rename preparation, saves and hierarchies
    `textDocument/rangeFormatting`, `textDocument/onTypeFormatting`,
    `textDocument/prepareRename`, `textDocument/willSave`, `textDocument/willSaveWaitUntil`,
    `textDocument/prepareCallHierarchy`, `callHierarchy/incomingCalls`,
    `callHierarchy/outgoingCalls`, `textDocument/prepareTypeHierarchy`,
    `typeHierarchy/supertypes` and `typeHierarchy/subtypes`. `Lsp.Connection#editing` is its
    client.

entry .LspAdvanced   the wire trait for semantic tokens, inlay hints, inline values, monikers and pull diagnostics
    `textDocument/semanticTokens/full`, `textDocument/semanticTokens/full/delta`,
    `textDocument/semanticTokens/range`, `textDocument/inlayHint`,
    `textDocument/inlineValue`, `textDocument/linkedEditingRange`, `textDocument/moniker`
    and `textDocument/diagnostic`. `Lsp.Connection#advanced` is its client.

entry .LspWorkspace   the wire trait for workspace symbols, commands, configuration and file operations
    `workspace/symbol`, `workspace/executeCommand`, `workspace/didChangeConfiguration`,
    `workspace/didChangeWatchedFiles`, `workspace/didChangeWorkspaceFolders`, the six
    `workspace/willCreateFiles`…`workspace/didDeleteFiles` methods, and `$/setTrace`.
    `Lsp.Connection#workspace` is its client.

entry .LspResolve   the wire trait for the `*/resolve` requests
    `completionItem/resolve`, `codeAction/resolve`, `codeLens/resolve`,
    `documentLink/resolve`, `inlayHint/resolve` and `workspaceSymbol/resolve`. Each takes
    the item itself as its `params`, not an object with named fields, so the parameter is
    marked `@bare`. `Lsp.Connection#resolve` is its client.

entry .LspSessional   the `Sessional` instance which opens a session with an `Lsp.Server`
  detail
      What `session` runs on an `Lsp.Server`: it mints the channel to the server — the pipes
      of a forked subprocess for `Server.Process`, the given streams for `Server.Streams` —
      starts a writer task draining the connection's outgoing messages onto it and a reader
      task routing what comes back, lends an `Lsp.Connection^` to the block, and cancels
      both tasks afterwards, aborting a subprocess rather than waiting for it. Its `Self` is
      `Lsp.Server` and its `Result` is `Lsp.Connection^`.

      The reader dispatches what the server sends to the `Lsp.Listener` the instance was
      built with: a notification the `Lsp.Client` trait models reaches the listener's named
      method; any other method is a request the server makes of its client, answered by
      `Listener#request`; a response is matched to the request awaiting it. The observer sees
      every message in both directions.

      The ambient instance is the given `Lsp.Server.sessional`, built from the listener and
      observer givens in scope; construct one explicitly only to pass a listener or observer
      as a value, and bind it as a local given:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment

      val tracing = new Lsp.Observer:
        def received(message: Text): Unit = Err.println(t"recv $message")
        def sent(message: Text): Unit = Err.println(t"send $message")

      supervise:
        given sessional: LspSessional^ = LspSessional(observer = tracing)
        Lsp.Server(sh"rust-analyzer").session: server ?=>
          server.initialize()
      ```

      It needs a `Monitor`, a `Probate`, `Diagnostics`, a `WorkingDirectory`, an
      `Environment` and a `Tactic[Lsp.Error]`: a subprocess which cannot be launched is
      reported as `Lsp.Error(Reason.Unstarted, details)` through the tactic. A malformed
      frame or a truncated stream is the reader's or the writer's fault, reported to the
      probate.
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Server
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Connection
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Listener
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Observer
  entry #Self   the subject: `Lsp.Server`
  entry #Result   what the session lends: `Lsp.Connection^`
  entry #session   opens the channel to a server, lends a connection to the block, and closes it after

entry .rewrite   the proxy's registration combinators: amend what a server upstream says
  detail
      A namespace of its own, so that `rewrite.hover` (amend a hover the server returned)
      and `Lsp.hover` (serve a hover) never collide. Each typed combinator is keyed by
      method, decodes the payload into the same `Lsp` type the server side uses, applies the
      function, and re-encodes; a method with no rewriter is forwarded untouched, and a
      payload which does not decode — the `null` a server sends for a hover it has none of
      — passes through as it stands, so a rewriter never spells out the empty case. Each
      needs an `Lsp.Proxy^` in scope: the block of `Lsp.proxy`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      Lsp.proxy(Lsp.Server(sh"rust-analyzer")):
        rewrite.capabilities(_.copy(hoverProvider = true))

        rewrite.hover: hover =>
          hover.copy(contents = Lsp.MarkupContent(value = t"_(proxied)_ ${hover.contents.value}"))

        rewrite.diagnostics(_.map { report => report.copy(message = t"[proxy] ${report.message}") })

        rewrite.outbound: (method, message) =>
          if method == t"textDocument/inlineValue" then Lsp.Transit.Drop else Lsp.Transit.Forward
      ```

      `.result` and `.notification` are the escape hatches for a method with no combinator,
      and `.outbound`, `.inbound` and `.connected` are the whole-message hooks. A hook runs on
      the thread which delivered the message it amends, which decides how it may use
      `upstream`: see `exegesis.upstream`.
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Proxy
  refer exegesis.Lsp.proxy
  refer exegesis.upstream
  refer exegesis.Lsp.Transit
  entry .capabilities   amends the capabilities the server upstream advertised
      Rewrites the `initialize` result: a proxy which adds a feature must claim it, and one
      which removes a feature must stop claiming it.
  entry .hover   amends each hover the server returns
  entry .completions   amends each completion list the server returns
  entry .definitions   amends each list of definition locations the server returns
  entry .symbols   amends each list of document symbols the server returns
  entry .codeActions   amends each list of code actions the server returns
  entry .codeLenses   amends each list of code lenses the server returns
  entry .inlayHints   amends each list of inlay hints the server returns
  entry .signatureHelp   amends each signature help the server returns
  entry .diagnostics   amends the diagnostics the server publishes
      The one notification among the typed rewriters: the `uri` and `version` the server
      reported are kept, and only the list of reports is the proxy's to change.
  entry .result   amends the result of any request, by method, as raw JSON
      `rewrite.result(method)(lambda)`: for a method this library does not model, or a
      payload a proxy would rather treat as JSON. The lambda takes a `Tactic[Json.Error]`
      from the proxy at each call, so a payload it cannot read passes through unchanged. A
      response carrying an `error` is left alone.
  entry .notification   amends the parameters of any notification the server sends, by method
      `rewrite.notification(method)(lambda)`, as `.result` for the `params` of a notification
      or of a request the server makes of its client; the message is rebuilt with its id, if
      it had one.
  entry .outbound   sees every message the editor sends, before it is forwarded
      `rewrite.outbound { (method, message) => … }`, returning a `Transit`: forward, rewrite,
      drop, or answer the request here so the server never sees it. Runs on the loop reading
      the editor, so a request to `upstream` may be awaited, though the editor waits with it.
  entry .inbound   sees every message the server sends, after any rewriter, before it is forwarded
      `rewrite.inbound { (method, message) => … }`: the method is that of a notification, or
      of the request a response answers, or `Unset`. Returns a `Transit`; `Answer` is ignored
      here. Runs on the task reading the server, which is the task that would deliver the
      answer to any request made of `upstream`, so such a request must be made in an `async`
      block, never awaited inline.
  entry .connected   runs a block once, on a task of its own, as soon as the session upstream is open
      The place for what a proxy must ask the server before it can amend anything, and the
      only hook free to await an answer. The loop reading the editor does not wait for it,
      since an editor's first request may arrive before a server has finished starting up.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      Lsp.proxy(Lsp.Server(sh"jdtls")):
        val classpath: Promise[Json] = Promise()

        rewrite.connected:
          classpath.offer(upstream.execute(t"java.project.getClasspaths").or(Json()))
      ```

entry .upstream   the proxy's session with the server upstream, for questions of its own
    `upstream` (needing an `Lsp.Proxy^` in scope) is the `Lsp.Connection` a proxy holds on
    the server it forwards to: the way a hook asks the server something the editor never
    asked, typically for project state only the server knows. Answers return to the caller
    which asked and never reach the editor. It is a method, not a value, because
    registration runs before the proxy has a server to talk to: calling it inside the
    registration block but outside a hook, before the session exists, is a panic.

    Which hook calls it decides whether it may await the answer. `rewrite.connected` has a
    task of its own and may await freely; `rewrite.outbound` runs on the loop reading the
    editor, so awaiting is safe but holds the editor up; `rewrite.inbound` runs on the task
    reading the server — the task that would deliver the response — so a request awaited
    there waits forever, and must be made in an `async` block.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    Lsp.proxy(Lsp.Server(sh"jdtls")):
      rewrite.outbound: (method, message) =>
        if method == t"workspace/executeCommand"
        then Lsp.Transit.Answer(upstream.execute(t"java.project.getClasspaths").or(Json()))
        else Lsp.Transit.Forward
    ```

entry .DemoLspServer   a complete, minimal language server, in `exegesis.demo`
    The `main` class of the `exegesis.demo` component: a resident daemon (ethereal's `cli`
    and `execute`, with the `completionsExecutive`) which calls `Lsp.listen` with four
    registrations — an `opened` handler publishing a warning over the whole document, a
    `hover` showing the word under the cursor, a fixed `complete()` list, and a
    `demo.reverse` command which reverses its text argument — and returns `Exit.Ok` when the
    editor closes the pipe. Point an editor at it as a language server for any language to
    see the protocol exercised end to end. Not exported to the `soundness` umbrella.

entry .DemoLspProxy   a complete, minimal proxy, in `exegesis.demo`
    A resident daemon which launches the language server named by its own arguments
    (`demo-proxy rust-analyzer`), forwards everything an editor sends it, and amends two of
    the answers on the way back: every hover is prefixed with _(proxied)_ and every
    published diagnostic is labelled `[demo]`, with `rewrite.capabilities` claiming hover in
    case the server did not. Everything else crosses unchanged, under the editor's own
    request ids. Not exported to the `soundness` umbrella.
